  {
    "title": "Demystifying Harrisburg",
    "url": "https://senatorlindseywilliams.com/demystifyinghbg/",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Demystifying Harrisburg\nHarrisburg can be needlessly– and sometimes purposely– complicated. My “Demystifying Harrisburg” event series is designed to cut through the noise and show you how your government can and should work for you.\nThese are some of my favorite events to host, because they tell you exactly how to get the most out of your government. Whether it’s tips on how to effectively lobby your legislator or learning how Pennsylvania’s Consumer Advocate protects you from utility scams, Demystifying Harrisburg will leave you feeling empowered and ready to take action.\nYou can watch previous installments of Demystifying Harrisburg below. Watch for upcoming installments in the series by\nsigning up for my newsletter\n!\nDemystifying Harrisburg: Elections\nDid you know that we have an election every 6 months? Learn about what Allegheny County does to prepare for every election, what you can expect at the polls, and how ballots are processed and counted.\nImportant Voting Resources:\n1-866-OUR-VOTE: Call with any questions about voting or to report any problems you experience while voting.\nRegister to Vote | Commonwealth of Pennsylvania (pa.gov)\n: Register to vote or update your voter registration.\nMail-in and Absentee Voting – Allegheny County, PA\n: Request your mail in ballot.\nBallot Return Sites – Allegheny County, PA\n: Allegheny County Ballot Return Sites\nIn-Person Voting – Allegheny County, PA\n: Allegheny County In- Person Voting Resources\nElections – Allegheny County, PA\n: Allegheny County Election Resources\nDemystifying Harrisburg: Office of Consumer Advocate\nPennsylvania’s Consumer Advocate protects your rights and makes sure you receive fair treatment from public utilities. They represent utility customers– people who use gas, water, and electric in their homes and businesses– before the Public Utility Commission and other courts and regulatory bodies.\nIn these discussions, we talked about your rights as a utility customer, how utilities increase their rates and the OCA’s role in that process, tips for shopping for an electricity provider, and more.\nYou can contact the Office of Consumer Advocate, including to sign up for updates about upcoming rate cases that affect you at:\nPhone: 1-800-684-6560\nEmail:\nconsumer@paoca.org\nWeb:\nPA Office of Consumer Advocate – Pennsylvania Office of Consumer Advocate\nOffice of the Consumer Advocate :: April 18, 2024\nMaking Utilities Work for You :: July 21, 2022\nDemystifying Harrisburg: Unionizing Your Workplace\nStanding up for your right to a fair wage, benefits, and a safe workplace is more important now than ever. In this edition of Demystifying Harrisburg, we gathered experienced union organizers and advocates to discuss how to organize a union and what your legal protections are as a worker under the National Labor Relations Act.\nNational Labor Relations Board:\nToll-Free number: 1-844-762-6572\nWebsite:\nNLRB\nEmail:\npublicinfo@nlrb.gov\nPennsylvania Labor Relations Board\n(412) 565-5318\nWebsite:\nPennsylvania Labor Relations Board | Department of Labor and Industry | Commonwealth of Pennsylvania\nDemystifying Harrisburg: How to Lobby Your Legislator\nPersonal stories about how the government impacts you are incredibly powerful. I’ve been fortunate enough to be entrusted with your personal stories and had the opportunity to share them on the Senate floor to underscore the people behind the work that we do.\nBut I can’t amplify your stories if you don’t tell them. How to Lobby Your Legislator covers how to feel more comfortable talking to your local legislative offices and how to make the most of your time when meeting with elected officials.\nWhether you’re navigating state or federal offices, your voice matters!\nFind your local representatives:\nFind My Legislator – The Official Website of the Pennsylvania General Assembly\nHow to Lobby Your Legislator :: April 20, 2022\nHow to Talk to Your Legislator :: March 25, 2021\nDemystifying Harrisburg: The Budget Process\nThe Pennsylvania General Assembly is constitutionally required to pass a budget every year by June 30th. In these installations of Demystifying Harrisburg, we cover the budget-making process, the governor’s role, a working timeline for the passage of the budget, and the budget as the foundation for the needed programs and services that Pennsylvania provides.\nPlease note that these two videos are overviews of the budget process, but they contain information specific to their fiscal years that is not applicable to every year.\nThe Budget :: February 17, 2022\nThe Budget Process :: June 29, 2021\nDemystifying Harrisburg: Requesting Information from State Government\nThe “Right to Know” Law in Pennsylvania ensures transparency by giving the public access to state and local government records. In this session of Demystifying Harrisburg, our experienced panelists explain how you can file a “Right to Know” request and discuss the process for a government to process and fulfill that request. We also have a conversation about how RTKs are used by the media to keep the public informed about government activity.\nDemystifying Harrisburg: Knowing the Rules and Tracking Legislation\nWhy does good legislation not get passed? Turns out, there’s a not-so-good reason for that: our Senate rules.\nJoin Senator Williams and her legislative staff for a deep dive on the legislative process– how a bill becomes a law and how the rules prevent even popular, bipartisan legislation from getting a fair shake. You can also learn how to research and track legislation that interests you.\nIf you are interested in tracking a certain bill, see:\nBills & Resolutions – The Official Website of the Pennsylvania General Assembly",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "A Pennsylvania state senator hosts an ongoing educational event series called 'Demystifying Harrisburg' that covers civic topics such as elections, utility consumer rights, union organizing, how to contact legislators, the state budget process, public records requests, and how the legislature works. The events feature guest speakers and provide residents with practical resources and contact information for relevant agencies. Past sessions are available to watch online, and new sessions are announced via newsletter.",
      "action_type": "event",
      "topics": [
        "voting_rights",
        "economy",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Transparency",
    "url": "https://senatorlindseywilliams.com/transparency/",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "TRANSPARENT AND ACCESSIBLE GOVERNMENT THAT WORKS FOR YOU\nView Legislation\nLatest News\nLegislation I’m Working On\nSenate Bill 838 − \tRequiring the Office of the Chief Clerk to Publicly Post All Legislators’ Expenses\nThis legislation that requires legislative expenses to be publicly posted online. Dollars spent by legislators are tax dollars and should be easily accessible by those who foot our bill.\nOftentimes, citizens or journalists who want to see how legislators spend their allotted budgets face obstacles in obtaining that information. This legislation will require that the Offices of the Chief Clerk of both the Pennsylvania House and Senate post the expenses of all legislators on a public facing website in a searchable format. Per diems, state vehicle usage, and reimbursements for expenses would be included on the website. The legislation will also require that all office, travel, and other expenses that come out of both legislators’ and caucus’ operating accounts be included on the website.\nWhile several legislators, myself included, already provide public access to expenses that come out of our office budgets on our websites, there are numerous other expenses that we are unable to account for because they come out of other pots of money. By requiring the Offices of the Chief Clerk to post all expenses, we will be able to provide the public will a full, transparent picture of how their taxpayer dollars are being spent. Additionally, by removing this responsibility from individual legislators, we will ensure a uniform, consistent process among offices.\nThe dollars we spend as legislators come from taxpayers. Taxpayers deserve transparency in knowing how their money is spent.\nLearn More\nE-Z Pass V-toll Notification\nThis legislation will require the Pennsylvania Turnpike Commission to notify E-Z Pass customers via email if they are charged with a “v-toll”. V-tolls are issued to customers when their E-Z Pass transponder is not detected – either because the transponder is not mounted properly or, despite proper placement, the transponder malfunctions due to age. In 2021, over 200,000 Pennsylvanians have been charged with v-tolls, totaling millions of dollars.\nUnfortunately, because E-Z Pass toll and fee charges are automatically withdrawn and because v-tolls have frequently been mislabeled on monthly statements, it has taken some customers months, or even years, to realize that they have been charged these fees. Customers may request an adjustment of their v-toll charges; however, the process can be difficult and adjustments are restricted to a period of 90 days, unless you can prove that the transponder malfunctioned. Currently, customers do not receive notifications that they are being charged a v-toll. Unless they are in the habit of regularly checking their E-Z Pass statement, these charges can go unnoticed and the 90-day adjustment window can be easily missed.\nAs an E-Z Pass user who regularly travels the Turnpike, I have received v-toll charges and had questions about what they were for or how to address them. The current system does not provide a transparent and reasonable way to notify users that they’re receiving extra charges, nor does it provide a clear explanation of how to dispute them.\nLeare More\nLatest News\nState lawmaker calls for a Congressional ban on insider trading and prediction market betting\nJune 23, 2026\nState Sen. Lindsey M. Williams (D-Allegheny) announced that she will introduce a resolution urging Congress to prohibit insider stock trading and betting in prediction markets for federal public officials. “For people to trust their elected officials, they have to...\nread more\nPennsylvania county bans poll workers from using election prediction markets\nApril 15, 2026\nA county in southeastern Pennsylvania has banned its poll workers from using prediction markets to bet on elections. The board of elections in Delaware County, outside of Philadelphia, recently approved a resolution that amended the oath poll workers take ahead of the...\nread more\nAlleged mishandling of pet cremations by Harrison funeral director spurs state legislation\nMarch 24, 2026\nTiffany Mantzouridis sat in the balcony of the state House chamber Monday, overwhelmed by emotion as legislators unanimously approved a bill to protect pet owners from what they called glaring oversights in cremation services. The Irwin resident is the whistleblower...\nread more\nDisclosure Of AI In Political Ads\nMarch 20, 2026\nHARRISBURG – Allegheny County Sen. Lindsey Williams is introducing legislation that will require political advertisements to disclose if artificial intelligence was used in their creation. Williams says voters deserve to know when images and videos that they are...\nread more\nPennsylvania Lawmakers Consider State Civil Rights Office As Federal Oversight Slows\nMarch 19, 2026\nPennsylvania lawmakers and state civil rights officials are racing to fill a hole left by the U.S. Department of Education after a wave of layoffs and regional office closures last year. Families in districts like Norwin say federal monitoring of school civil rights...\nread more\nWith less federal oversight, what’s next for civil rights enforcement in Pennsylvania schools?\nMarch 19, 2026\nWhen Norwin School District, about 40 minutes east of Pittsburgh, signed an agreement with the U.S. Department of Education’s Office of Civil Rights (OCR) in September 2024, the district agreed to take several actions to fix what the department called a “racially...\nread more\nPennsylvania senators propose requiring disclosure of AI in campaign ads\nMarch 18, 2026\nHARRISBURG, Pa. — In an election year, attack ads are nothing new. What is new, however, are ads depicting candidates using artificial intelligence. The use of AI in political campaigns is drawing backlash, and in Pennsylvania, it’s now drawing a proposal to scale it...\nread more\nAI political ads are here, and a Pennsylvania lawmaker wants to make sure voters know\nMarch 18, 2026\nHARRISBURG, Pa. (WHTM) — Campaigns frequently exaggerate political ads, but as Pennsylvania heads into election season, voters might not even know if what they’re watching even happened at all. In artificial intelligence’s quest to touch all aspects of society, it...\nread more\nDo political ads have to disclose AI usage? PA bill could require makers to do so\nMarch 18, 2026\nread more\nPA Sen. looks to ban insider stock trading for Congress, President of United States\nMarch 14, 2026\nPENNSYLVANIA (WTAJ) — A Pennsylvania Senator is looking to ban insider stock trading and participation in prediction markets. “Public officials should not be able to make money off non-public information provided to them in service of their elected position,” Sen....\nread more\nFamilies turn to states for civil rights support as Trump dismantles the Education Department\nMarch 5, 2026\nWASHINGTON — In their mostly white school district, Black students routinely heard racial slurs. White classmates hurled insults like \"slave,\" \"monkey\" or worse. It often went unpunished. Parents made those claims in a 2024 complaint asking the U.S. Education...\nread more\nFalse claims laws in other states have brought in millions. Why hasn’t it passed in Pa.?\nFebruary 23, 2026\nHARRISBURG — For more than two decades, state lawmakers from both parties have pushed for a new law to crack down on fraudulent government spending and encourage whistleblowers to come forward. Each time, powerful health care and business interests stunted those...\nread more\nSpringdale demonstrators plead for detained immigrant Cordova Flores, as confrontation ensues\nFebruary 14, 2026\nA Valentine’s Day rally drew about 200 demonstrators to Springdale, where speakers decried the immigration arrest of Randy Cordova Flores and called for a review of the borough’s relationship with immigration authorities. But while Cordova Flores’ supporters spoke...\nread more\nLocal lawmakers question ICE’s detention of Springdale father\nFebruary 12, 2026\nThough local officials have mostly remained silent,the Alle-Kiski Valley’s lawmakers are seeking more details after a traffic stop in Springdale ended with a local father in ICE custody on Tuesday. Randy Cordova-Flores, 36, remains in custody at Northern Regional...\nread more\nSharpsburg continues to rack up funding for multimillion-dollar Allegheny Shores development\nFebruary 11, 2026\nTransformation of the Sharpsburg riverfront continues to chug along, with officials shoring up streetscape plans expected to improve safety and access to the site. Councilman Roman Mason believes work near the 19th Street entrance of the proposed Allegheny Shores...\nread more\nLawmakers, pet advocates push bill spurred by pet cremation scandal\nJanuary 22, 2026\nState Sen. Nick Pisciottano said the state’s framework for pet cremation oversight is “a little like the Wild West.” The legislator, whose district includes Plum, Monroe­ville and parts of the Mon Valley, proposed Senate Bill 950 to strengthen consumer protection and...\nread more\nProtecting Whistleblowers and Taxpayer Money:  Senators Lindsey M. Williams and Kristin Phillips-Hill Reintroduce Commonwealth Fraud Prevention Act\nJanuary 31, 2023\n(Harrisburg, PA) January 31, 2023 --- Senator Lindsey M. Williams (D-Allegheny) and Senator Kristin Phillips-Hill (R-York) announced today that they plan to reintroduce the Commonwealth Fraud Prevention Act for Taxpayer Accountability (CFPA) this session. This...\nread more\nSen. Muth, Sen. Williams Highlight Need for Senate Rules Reforms at Virtual Roundtable\nDecember 9, 2022\nHARRISBURG, December 9, 2022 – State Senator Katie Muth (D- Berks/Chester/Montgomery), Chair of the Senate Democratic Policy Committee, and state Senator Lindsey Williams (D-Allegheny) hosted a virtual roundtable discussion Wednesday night on the operating rules of...\nread more\nSenator Lindsey M. Williams Questions Decision to Shift Responsibility for Public School Audits\nMarch 31, 2022\nPittsburgh, Pa. − March 31, 2022 − Today, Senator Lindsey M. Williams sent a letter to Auditor General Timothy DeFoor and Department of Education Secretary Noe Ortega in response to last week’s announcement by the Auditor General that he will be closing the Bureau of...\nread more\nPa Senate Democrats Call on Republican Colleagues to Take Action on Government Transparency and Accountability Measures\nSeptember 22, 2021\nHarrisburg, Pa. − September 22, 2021 − Senators Lindsey M. Williams (D-Allegheny), Katie Muth (D-Chester, Montgomery, and Berks), Amanda M. Cappelletti (D-Montgomery and Delaware), John Kane (D-Chester and Delaware), Tim Kearney (D-Chester and Delaware), and Judy...\nread more",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "State Senator Lindsey Williams introduced two pieces of legislation: Senate Bill 838, which would require the Pennsylvania House and Senate to publicly post all legislator expenses online in a searchable format, and a separate bill requiring the Pennsylvania Turnpike Commission to notify E-Z Pass customers by email when they are charged a v-toll fee. Her office page also lists several other legislative proposals she is working on, including requiring AI disclosure in political ads and a resolution urging Congress to ban insider trading by federal officials.",
      "action_type": "bill_introduced",
