  {
    "title": "Senator Lindsey M. Williams Announces Over $9.8 Million in Local Share Account Funding for Community Needs",
    "url": "https://senatorlindseywilliams.com/senator-lindsey-m-williams-announces-over-9-8-million-in-local-share-account-funding-for-community-needs/",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Senator Lindsey M. Williams Announces Over $9.8 Million in Local Share Account Funding for Community Needs\nSeptember 15, 2026\nPittsburgh, Pa.\n−\nSeptember 15, 2026\n− In partnership with state representatives, Senator Lindsey M. Williams announced more than $9.8 million in Local Share Account grant funding for projects. These projects will improve the daily quality of life for people across Senate District 38 by supporting library access, local water infrastructure, municipal safety, neighborhood connectivity, and first responder needs.\n“The funding announced today will make a real difference in the quiet systems that we rely on every day, but often don’t notice until something is wrong,” said Senator Williams. “These grants will also replace aging waterlines and update water infrastructure throughout the 38\nth\nSenate District, ensuring that residents have access to the clean, pure water guaranteed to them by the Pennsylvania Constitution. They will help first responders, like the City of Pittsburgh, Ross West View EMS, and Richland EMS, purchase the vehicles and equipment they need to respond quickly and efficiently to emergencies, allowing them to be there for us when we need them the most. They will replace aging municipal equipment, allowing our public works departments to better take care of our roads and parks, with lower maintenance costs for local taxpayers. And the funding for a new Bookmobile will connect seniors and new patrons with the joy of the library, all across Allegheny County. I’m proud to help secure state investments in the projects that improve the quality of life for everyone.”\nProjects receiving LSA funding in Senate District 38 are:\nThe Allegheny County Library Association\nwill receive $338,071 to purchase a new Bookmobile.\nALCOSAN\nwill receive $300,000 to purchase a new combination sewer cleaning truck to add to its existing fleet.\nAllegheny Health Network\nwill receive $1,000,000 to construct an EMS Training and Resource Center at their Wexford Hospital campus in Pine Township.\nAspinwall Borough\nwill receive $34,327 for improvements at Aspinwall Community Park, including new pickleball courts and a multiuse court.\nCheswick Borough\nwill receive $300,000 for roadway reconstruction on Maple Street, South Highland Avenue, Oak Street, and Railroad Avenue.\nEast Deer Township\nwill receive $400,000 to replace waterlines along West 8\nth\nand West 9\nth\nHampton Township\nwill receive $200,000 to replace the roofs of two buildings within the Municipal administration complex.\nHarmar Water Authority\nwill receive $500,000 to install a second filter unit at the water treatment plant.\nHarrison Township\nwill receive $600,000 to complete a sanitary sewer inspection and cleaning project through the Township.\nIngomar North Recreation and Swimming Club\nwill receive $200,000 to replace their pool liner, install a new pool vacuum, purchase a new pool cover, pave the parking lot and basketball court, and renovate the baby pool and storage deck.\nThe Kelly Strayhorn Theater\nwill receive $300,000 to replace mechanical and HVAC systems at their new facility.\nMcCandless Township\nwill receive $179,457 for repairs to the LaRoche East Dam.\nNorthern Regional Police Officers Association\nwill receive $93,577 to purchase two fully equipped police vehicles.\nO’Hara Township\nwill receive $100,000 to install a retaining wall along River Road to prevent potential landslides.\nO’Hara Township\nwill receive $200,000 to stabilize the Sycamore Run stream bank and restore pedestrian access to the Salamander and O’Hara Trails.\nO’Hara Township\nwill receive $50,000 to install 13,500 square feet of sidewalks and ADA ramps along Alpha Drive.\nPine Township\nwill receive $250,000 for the construction of a new municipal building on Pine Park Drive.\nThe City of Pittsburgh\nwill receive $800,000 to purchase a new pumper fire truck.\nThe Pittsburgh Harlequins Rugby Football Association\nwill receive $200,000 to install a high-efficiency LED sports lighting system at Founder’s Field in Indiana Township.\nPittsburgh Water and Sewer Authority\nwill receive $200,000 to rehabilitate the west raw water intake at their water treatment plant.\nRichland EMS\nwill receive $197,000 to purchase a new ambulance.\nRichland Township\nwill receive $200,000 to purchase a 10-ton dump truck equipped with a plow and salt spreader. They will also receive $125,000 to replace 1200 linear feet of waterline along Beechnut Drive.\nRoss West View EMS\nAuthority\nwill receive $100,000 to purchase a new ambulance.\nShaler Township\nwill receive $200,000 to construct a new ADA-accessible trail at Fall Run Park.\nSharpsburg Borough\nwill receive $200,000 to replace sidewalks along 13\nth\nStreet and Canal Street.\nSouthern Allegheny Valley Emergency Services (SAVES)\nwill receive $150,000 to modernize their fire station, including the addition of a weather relief center that will provide residents with a safe, accessible, and climate-controlled area for use during extreme weather events and prolonged power outages.\nWest Deer Township\nwill receive $434,800 for improvements at Bairdford Park, including building a new centralized concession stand and converting the existing storage building into a kitchen for pavilion use.\nWest View Borough\nwill receive $57,169 to replace the public stairway along Martsolf Avenue and Center Avenue, and $75,000 to replace the public stairway along Ridgewood-Center Avenue.\nWest View Water Authority\nwill receive $600,000 to replace the water line on Thompson Run Road between Halzett and McIntyre Roads.\nSenator Williams partnered with State Representatives Aerion Abney (D-19), Emily Kinkead (D-20), Lindsay Powell (D-21), Dan Frankel (D-23), La’Tasha D. Mayes (D-24), Jeremy Shaffer (R-28), Arvind Venkat (D-30), and Mandy Steele (D-33) to bring this funding into local neighborhoods.\nSenator Williams partnered with Senators Jay Costa and Wayne Fontana and Allegheny County State Representatives Aerion Abney (D-19), Emily Kinkead (D-20), Lindsay Powell (D-21), Dan Frankel (D-23), La’Tasha D. Mayes (D-24), Dan Deasy (D-27), Abigail Salisbury (D-34), Jessica Benham (D-36), and John Inglis (D-38) to fund the City of Pittsburgh firetruck award and the ALCOSAN sewer cleaning truck award.\nLSA Grants are administered by the Commonwealth Financing Authority (CFA) and are funded through the PA Race Horse Development and Gaming Act. These funds support projects in the public interest. Additional information about the LSA program can be found\nhere\n.\nSenator Williams is committed to working alongside the people and organizations of Senate District 38 to build community through increasing state investment, demonstrating that government can and should work for people and improve their quality of life. For information on available grants, or for help navigating state grant and award programs, please\ncontact her office\n.\n###",
